{"title":"AP EAMCET","category":"Engineering","bank":"ap_eamcet","subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Mathematical Induction","count":8,"questions":[{"id":9757,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Mathematical Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"AP EAMCET","q":"If $2^{\\mathrm{n}}$ divides 16 ! and $2^{\\mathrm{n}+1}$ does not divide 16 !, then $\\mathrm{n}=$","options":["14","15","16","17"],"answer":1,"solution":"Since $2^n$ divides 16 ! and $2^{n+1}$ does not divide 16 !.\n$\\therefore n=$ Number of times 2 occurs in $16 !=\\left[\\frac{16}{2}\\right]+\\left[\\frac{16}{2^2}\\right]+\\left[\\frac{16}{2^3}\\right]+\\left[\\frac{16}{2^4}\\right]=8+4+2+1=15$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"AP EAMCET 2023 (16 May Shift 1)","_marksId":"65c21cd2ae468f13689744a3"},{"id":9758,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Mathematical Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"AP EAMCET","q":"Using mathematical induction, the numbers <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math>'s are defined by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo> </mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mfenced><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo></math>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>"],"answer":1,"solution":"<p>Given, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo> </mo><mfenced><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p>Putting, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>, </p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>19</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><mo> </mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced><mo> </mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mo>⇒</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>⇒</mo><mn>37</mn><mo> </mo></math>, (wrong option)</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced><mo> </mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>⇒</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>⇒</mo><mn>19</mn><mo> </mo></math>, (right option)</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>)</mo><mo> </mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⇒</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn><mo> </mo></math>(wrong option)</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mn>4</mn></mfenced><mo> </mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⇒</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⇒</mo><mn>36</mn><mo> </mo></math>(wrong option)</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"AP EAMCET 2021 (20 Aug Shift 2)","_marksId":"65c21b70ae468f13689738b9"},{"id":9759,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Mathematical Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"AP EAMCET","q":"For what natural numbers <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>N</mi></math>, the inequality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>></mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math> is valid?","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>N</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mo>{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>}</mo></math>"],"answer":1,"solution":"<p>Given that, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>></mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>></mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>⇒</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>></mo><mn>2</mn></math> is not true</p><p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>></mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>⇒</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>></mo><mn>3</mn></math> is True.</p><p>So, Given inequality is true for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math>.</p><p>Assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>></mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math> is true for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mo>></mo><mn>2</mn></math>.</p><p>But <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>></mo><mn>1</mn></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mo>></mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>></mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>></mo><mfenced><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p>Hence, the inequality holds for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p>It follows by mathematical induction that the given inequality holds for all natural numbers <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math>.</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"AP EAMCET 2021 (19 Aug Shift 2)","_marksId":"65c21b4cae468f1368973779"},{"id":9760,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Mathematical Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"AP EAMCET","q":"Let \\(P(n): 2+2^2+2^3+\\ldots+2^n=2^{n+1}, n \\in \\mathbf{N}\\). Then,","options":["\\(P(m)\\) is true \\(\\Rightarrow P(m+1)\\) is true","\\(P(n)\\) is true for all \\(n \\in \\mathrm{N}\\)","\\(P(n)\\) is true for all \\(n \\geq 20\\)","\\(P(n)\\) is true for all \\(n \\leq 10\\)"],"answer":0,"solution":"Given,\n\\(p(n)=2+2^2+2^3+\\ldots .+2^n=2^{n+1}\\)\nWhere \\(n \\in \\mathrm{N}\\).\nLet \\(p(m)\\) is true then,\n\\(P(m)=2+2^2+2^3+\\ldots .+2^m=2^{m+1}\\)\nSo,\n\\(\\begin{aligned}\nP(m+1) & =2+2^2+2^3+\\ldots .+2^m+2^{m+1} \\\\\n& =\\left(2+2^2+2^3+\\ldots .+2^m\\right)+2^{m+1} \\\\\n& =2^{(m+1)}+2^{m+1}=2.2^{m+1}=2^{m+2}\n\\end{aligned}\\)\nClearly, if \\(P(m)\\) is true then \\(p(m+1)\\) is also true.\n\\(\\therefore P(m)\\) is true \\(\\Rightarrow P(m+1)\\) is true.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"AP EAMCET 2020 (17 Sep Shift 2)","_marksId":"65c21aafae468f1368973221"},{"id":9761,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Mathematical Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"AP EAMCET","q":"$\\sum_{n=1}^5 n\\left(n^2+n+1\\right)=$","options":["500","155","295","395"],"answer":2,"solution":"No solution. Refer to answer key.","difficulty":"Easy","source":"AP EAMCET 2018 (24 Apr Shift 2)","_marksId":"65c219f1ae468f1368972b98"},{"id":9762,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Mathematical Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"AP EAMCET","q":"If $2\\left(4^{2 n+1}\\right)+3^{3 n+1}$ is divisible by $k, k\\gt 1$ for all $n \\in N$, then the value of $k$ is","options":["19","17","11","13"],"answer":2,"solution":"The given expression<br />$\\begin{aligned} & 2\\left(4^{2 n+1}\\right)+3^{3 n+1}=P(n) \\\\ & \\because \\quad P(n)=8\\left(4^{2 n}\\right)+3\\left(3^{3 n}\\right) \\\\ & \\text {For } n=1 \\text {, } \\\\ & \\because \\quad n \\in N \\\\ & P(n=1)=128+81=209 \\\\ & \\end{aligned}$<br />is divisible by 19 and 11.<br />Similarly, $\\quad P(n=2)=2048+2187=4235$ is divisible by 11 but not divisible by 19.<br />So, the given expression $2\\left(4^{2 n+1}\\right)+3^{3 n+1}$ is divisible by<br />For all<br />$k=11$<br />$n \\in N \\text {. }$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"AP EAMCET 2018 (23 Apr Shift 2)","_marksId":"65c219cdae468f1368972a58"},{"id":9763,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Mathematical Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"AP EAMCET","q":"$\\sum_{k=1}^{2 n+1}(-1)^{k-1} \\cdot k^2$ equals to","options":["$(n-1)(2 n-1)$","$(n+1)(2 n+1)$","$(n+1)(2 n-1)$","$(n-1)(2 n+1)$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\begin{aligned} & \\sum_{k=1}^{2 n+1}(-1)^{n-1} k^2 \\\\ & =\\left[1^2-2^2+3^2-4^2+\\ldots-(2 n)^2+(2 n+1)^2\\right] \\\\ & =\\left[1^2+3^2+5^2+\\ldots+(2 n+1)^2\\right] \\\\ & -\\left[2^2+4^2+6^2+\\ldots+(2 n)^2\\right] \\\\ & =\\left[1^2+2^2+3^2+4^2+\\ldots+(2 n+1)^2\\right] \\\\ & -2\\left[2^2+4^2+6^2+\\ldots+(2 n)^2\\right] \\\\ & =\\sum(2 n+1)^2-2 \\times 2^2\\left[1+2^2+3^2+\\ldots+n^2\\right] \\\\ & =\\frac{(2 n+1)(2 n+1+1)\\{2(2 n+1)+1\\}}{6}-\\sum n^2 \\\\ & {\\left[\\because \\sum n^2=\\frac{n(n+1)(2 n+1)}{6}\\right]} \\\\ & =\\frac{(2 n+1)(2 n+2)(4 n+3)}{6} \\\\ & -8 \\frac{n(n+1)(2 n+1)}{6} \\\\ & =\\frac{(n+1)(2 n+1)}{6}[2(4 n+3)-8 n] \\\\ & =\\frac{(n+1)(2 n+1)}{6}[8 n+6-8 n] \\\\ & =(n+1)(2 n+1) \\\\ & \\end{aligned}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"AP EAMCET 2014","_marksId":"65c218f7ae468f13689722fd"},{"id":9764,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Mathematical Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"AP EAMCET","q":"Using mathematical induction, the numbers $a_n$ 's are defined by,\n$a_0=1, a_{n+1}=3 n^2+n+a_n,(n \\geq 0) .$\nThen, $a_n$ is equal to","options":["$n^3+n^2+1$","$n^3-n^2+1$","$n^3-n^2$","$n^3+n^2$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\begin{aligned}\n& \\text { Given, } a_0=1, a_{n+1}=3 n^2+n+a_n \\\\\n& \\Rightarrow \\quad a_1=3(0)+0+a_0=1 \\\\\n& \\text { and } \\quad a_2=3(1)^2+1+a_1=3+1+1=5\n\\end{aligned}$\nFrom option (b),\n$\\begin{array}{ll}\n\\text { Let } & P(n)=n^3-n^2+1 \\\\\n\\therefore & P(0)=0-0+1=1=a_0 \\\\\n\\text { and } & P(1)=1^3-1^2+1=1=a_1 \\\\\n& P(2)=(2)^3-(2)^2+1=5=a_2\n\\end{array}$\nHence, option (b) is correct.","difficulty":"Easy","source":"AP EAMCET 2009","_marksId":"65c21899ae468f1368971fb8"}]}